window.SEARCH_INDEX={"pages": [{"p": "week-01.html", "s": 0, "t": "What is AI?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Artificial intelligence is the field of building computer systems that do tasks which normally need human intelligence: understanding language, recognising images, making decisions and learning from experience. It is a goal, a field of study and a family of very different tools, from your spam filter to ChatGPT. WATCH WATCH · CRASHCOURSE"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 1, "t": "Is it AI or not?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Sort each item, then reveal the answers. Notice the pattern: the items that count as AI learn from data or adapt, while the others follow one fixed rule. SORT THE ITEMS"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 2, "t": "Four ways to define AI", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Russell and Norvig sort definitions of AI by two questions: should a machine think or act, and should it copy humans or be ideally rational? That gives four views. Most modern AI follows the fourth, acting rationally: choosing the best action to reach a goal. THE 2×2 · CLICK EACH VIEW"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 3, "t": "AI vs ordinary software", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Ordinary software follows rules a programmer wrote: rules plus data give answers. Machine learning turns this around: data plus answers give the rules. Change the rule filter on the right and try to catch every spam email without blocking the free pizza announcement. RULE FILTER VS LEARNING FILTER EMAIL SUBJECT REALLY RULE FILTER LEARNING FILTER FREE MONEY waiting for you!!! spam BLOCKED ✓ BLOCKED ✓ F-R-E-E m0ney, claim now spam INBOX ✗ BLOCKED ✓ Your Nol card top-up receipt normal INBOX ✓ INBOX ✓ You won a prize, send your bank details spam INBOX ✗ BLOCKED ✓ Meeting moved to 3 pm normal INBOX ✓ INBOX ✓ Limited offer: click to claim your reward spam INBOX ✗ BLOCKED ✓ GED 102 quiz grades are posted normal INBOX ✓ INBOX ✓ Free pizza at the student club today normal INBOX ✓ INBOX ✓"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 4, "t": "Which fields built AI?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "AI did not come from computer science alone. Philosophy, mathematics, statistics, neuroscience, linguistics, psychology and economics each gave it a key idea, which is why AI is called interdisciplinary. EIGHT FOUNDATION FIELDS"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 5, "t": "Is AI a real science?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "AI is both a science and an engineering discipline. It tests hypotheses and measures results on shared benchmarks, but much of it is building tools that work before we fully understand why, and black-box models make some results hard to explain or reproduce. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 6, "t": "Can a computer be smarter than a human?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "All AI today is narrow: superhuman at one task and helpless at everything else. General AI does not exist yet, and super AI is theoretical. Moravec's paradox explains the gap: what is hard for us, like chess, is easy for machines, and what is easy for a child, like recognising a friend, is hard for them. THREE KINDS OF AI · THEN SORT THE TASKS"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 7, "t": "Train your own AI in five minutes", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Teachable Machine lets you train an image model in the browser with your webcam. Teach it two objects, test it with a third it has never seen, and watch it still pick one of the two with confidence: it only knows what its data showed it. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 8, "t": "Can a neural network guess your doodle?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Quick, Draw! asks you to sketch six objects in 20 seconds each while a neural network guesses what you are drawing. It learned from millions of doodles drawn by players around the world. At the end, open a drawing it missed and compare yours with the examples it learned from. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 9, "t": "Case: Alan Turing asks “Can machines think?”", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "In 1950 the British mathematician Alan Turing published “Computing Machinery and Intelligence”. Instead of defining thinking, he proposed a test: if a judge chatting by text cannot tell the machine from a human, the machine passes. The question still drives debates about ChatGPT today. ALAN TURING, AGED 16"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 10, "t": "Behaviour or understanding?", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "In 1980 the philosopher John Searle replied with the Chinese Room: a person who knows no Chinese follows a rule book and gives perfect Chinese answers, yet understands nothing. Turing judges behaviour; Searle says behaviour is not understanding. Vote, then defend your answer with one of the two. TURING TEST VS CHINESE ROOM"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 11, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "This week's ideas continue in IBM's free Introduction to Artificial Intelligence course, the first step towards the AI Fundamentals badge. Finish it, keep your proof, and send it for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 12, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 1 · Introduction to AI", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Before Week 2, read RB a, Chapters 1–3 (Mitchell), and pick one moment in AI history that interests you: it could become Assignment 1. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Definition Four views AI vs software Foundations Kinds of AI Tests Tasks that need human intelligence A goal · a field · many tools 1. Which is the best exam definition of AI? 2. In machine learning, what goes in and what comes out? 3. Most modern AI follows which of the four views? 4. All AI that exists today is… 5. Searle's Chinese Room argues that… 6. Moravec's paradox says that…"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 0, "t": "When did AI begin?", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "ChatGPT looks new, but it is the latest chapter of an 80-year story. Vote for the year you think AI began as a named field, then see the answer. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 1, "t": "The roots: from ideas to a named field", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "A mathematical neuron in 1943, Turing's question in 1950, and the 1956 Dartmouth workshop, where John McCarthy's proposal named the field “artificial intelligence”. Click the dots: the line shows how excitement and funding rose and fell for eighty years. 80 YEARS OF AI · CLICK THE DOTS"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 2, "t": "Early AI: rules and symbols", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Early AI was symbolic: experts wrote IF-THEN rules and the computer followed them, with no learning from data. Try the expert system on the right with an unusual patient, and watch it fall silent or fire two rules that disagree. EXPERT SYSTEM · TRY UNUSUAL CASES"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 3, "t": "AI winters: when hype met reality", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Researchers promised human-level AI within a generation. When slow computers, scarce data and brittle rules could not deliver, funding collapsed twice: in the 1970s and again in the late 1980s. These periods are called AI winters. THE TWO WINTERS First winter · 1970s Second winter · late 1980s to early 1990s What ended the winters?"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 4, "t": "Why deep learning took off in 2012", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Neural networks existed since the perceptron in 1958, and backpropagation showed how to train them in 1986. They only took off around 2012, when three ingredients finally met: big data, fast graphics chips and better algorithms. WHY 2012?"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 5, "t": "Put AI history in order", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Click the milestones from oldest to newest. Wrong clicks shake and are counted, so try for zero mistakes. TIMELINE GAME"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 6, "t": "Talk to the first chatbot (1966)", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "ELIZA, written by Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT, matched keywords and turned your sentences back into questions. Some users still believed it understood them. Chat with it for five minutes and find the rules behind its replies. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 7, "t": "See how a chess engine thinks", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Deep Blue won by searching millions of positions per second. Lichess lets you run Stockfish, a modern engine, in your browser. Make a few moves, switch on computer analysis, and watch it score the position and list the lines it is searching. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 8, "t": "Case: Deep Blue (1997) vs AlphaGo (2016)", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Deep Blue beat Kasparov at chess with expert-written scoring and brute-force search. Go has more possible positions than there are atoms in the observable universe, so search alone cannot win it. AlphaGo learned from human games and then from playing itself, and beat Lee Sedol in 2016. HOW MANY POSSIBLE POSITIONS?"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 9, "t": "Watch: the match that surprised experts", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Watch the opening of the AlphaGo film in class. Note the moment experts called a move “creative”, and ask yourself: can a learned move be creative if no human taught it? WATCH WATCH · GOOGLE DEEPMIND"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 10, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Microsoft Learn's Introduction to AI concepts module turns this history into today's vocabulary. Finish it, keep the trophy, and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 11, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 2 · The History of AI", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Assignment 1, “Where it all began”, uses today's timeline: the brief, rubric and submission button are on the course page. Before Week 3, read RB f, Chapter 2. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Roots Symbolic AI AI winters Deep learning AI spring 1943 mathematical neuron 1950 Turing’s question 1956 Dartmouth names AI 1958 perceptron · 1966 ELIZA 1. AI became a named field at… 2. Symbolic AI works by… 3. An “AI winter” is… 4. Which three ingredients made deep learning take off around 2012? 5. AlphaGo differed from Deep Blue because it… 6. The 2017 Transformer paper matters because…"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 0, "t": "Be the language model", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "Before any theory, play the machine. Guess the missing word in each sentence, then open the answer. You just did what a language model does: predict the most likely next word from patterns you have seen before. GUESS THE NEXT WORD"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 1, "t": "AI, machine learning and deep learning", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "The three terms nest inside each other. AI is the whole field. Machine learning is the part of AI that learns from data. Deep learning is the part of machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers. CLICK EACH RING"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 2, "t": "Three ways a machine learns", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "Supervised learning learns from labelled examples, like emails marked spam. Unsupervised learning finds groups with no labels, like customer types. Reinforcement learning learns by trial and error with rewards, like AlphaGo. Sort the examples on the right. WHICH TYPE OF LEARNING?"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 3, "t": "Watch one neuron learn", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "A neural network is built from simple units. Each one multiplies its inputs by weights, adds them up, and fires if the total passes a threshold. Training means nudging the weights after every mistake. Press “Train 1 round” a few times and watch the dividing line move until it separates the two groups. WATCH ONE NEURON LEARN"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 4, "t": "How ChatGPT writes a reply", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "ChatGPT cuts your text into tokens, uses attention to decide which earlier tokens matter, predicts a likely next token, adds it, and repeats until the reply is done. Play the flow, then try the tokeniser and attention demo below it. TOKENS AND ATTENTION"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 5, "t": "Three stages of training", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "First, pre-training on a huge amount of text teaches the model to predict the next word. Then fine-tuning on example conversations teaches it to follow instructions. Finally, human feedback rewards the answers people prefer. PRE-TRAINING → FINE-TUNING → RLHF"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 6, "t": "Train a neural network in your browser", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "TensorFlow Playground shows a real neural network learning live. Pick a dataset, press play, and watch the coloured background change as the weights update. Then remove neurons and layers until it can no longer learn the spiral. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 7, "t": "Look inside a real transformer", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "Transformer Explainer runs a small GPT-2 model in your browser. Type a sentence and follow it through tokens, attention and the final probabilities for the next word. Move the temperature slider and watch the choices become safer or more surprising. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 8, "t": "Case: when the model invents facts", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "In 2023 a New York judge fined two lawyers who had filed a legal brief citing court cases that did not exist: ChatGPT had invented them. A language model predicts likely text, not true text. This is called hallucination, and the fix is human checking. REAL CASE IN THE NEWS · REUTERS · 22 JUNE 2023"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 9, "t": "Which model fits the job?", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "Different problems need different algorithms: regression predicts numbers, clustering finds groups, convolutional networks read images and transformers handle language. Choose the right tool for each real case. WHICH MODEL WOULD YOU USE? 1. Group 10,000 customers into types, with no labels 2. Detect pneumonia on chest X-rays 3. Translate Arabic to English 4. Predict tomorrow's electricity demand in kWh"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 10, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "IBM's free Natural Language Processing and Computer Vision course goes deeper into how machines read text and see images. It is course 2 of the AI Fundamentals badge. Finish it and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 11, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 3 · How AI Thinks – Smart Tools, Not Magic", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Assignment 1 is due this week: the brief and submission button are on the course page. Before Week 4, read RB a, Chapter 13 and RB b, Chapter 7. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Nesting Types of ML Neural networks Key models ChatGPT Training Deep learning ⊂ ML ⊂ AI 1. Which statement is correct? 2. Grouping customers without any labels is… 3. During training, a neural network… 4. What does “attention” do in a transformer? 5. In pre-training, a language model mainly learns by… 6. RLHF stands for…"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 0, "t": "AI is already in your pocket", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Take out your phone and tick every AI feature you used today. Face unlock, autocorrect, photo search, maps and your feeds are all narrow AI making predictions from data, most of the time without you noticing. PHONE AI HUNT"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 1, "t": "How your feed decides what you see", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "A recommender collects what you watch, like and skip, compares you with similar users, predicts your next click and fills your feed with it. Your clicks then feed the next round, so the loop can narrow what you see into a filter bubble. Click a few videos below and watch your feed change. YOUR FEED · CLICK WHAT YOU LIKE"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 2, "t": "Social equity: four lenses", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Social equity asks whether AI's benefits and harms are shared fairly. Four lenses help: who has access, who is represented in the data, who is affected by decisions, and who has a voice in how systems are built. FOUR LENSES · CLICK EACH"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 3, "t": "Who benefits from the AI revolution?", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "AI creates winners and losers at the same time. People with skills, data and capital tend to gain first, while routine jobs and groups missing from the data carry more of the risk. Where do you expect to stand? WHERE DO YOU STAND?"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 4, "t": "See what Google thinks you like", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Google's My Ad Center shows the topics, brands and personal details it uses to choose your ads. Sign in with your own account, compare its guesses with reality, and switch off one topic you do not want to be targeted by. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · GOOGLE ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 5, "t": "Build a hiring algorithm, then watch it go wrong", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Survival of the Best Fit is a short game made by students at NYU Abu Dhabi. You hire people by hand, then hand the job to a machine that learns from your past choices. Play it to the end and explain where the bias came from. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 6, "t": "Case: a risk score in court", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "COMPAS scores defendants in some US courts on how likely they are to reoffend. In 2016 ProPublica found that Black defendants who did not reoffend were almost twice as likely as white defendants to be wrongly labelled high risk. The data reflected past policing, and the model learned it. COMPAS · FALSE “HIGH RISK” LABELS"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 7, "t": "Case: the hiring tool that learned to prefer men", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Amazon built a tool to rate job applicants, trained on ten years of CVs that came mostly from men. It learned to mark down CVs that included the word “women's”, and Amazon dropped the project. It is the real version of the game you just played. REAL CASE IN THE NEWS · REUTERS VIA CNBC · 10 OCTOBER 2018"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 8, "t": "Watch: “the coded gaze”", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Joy Buolamwini found that face-analysis software failed to see her face until she wore a white mask. Watch her talk and note the three steps she proposes for fairer code. WATCH WATCH · TED · JOY BUOLAMWINI"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 9, "t": "Debate: are the disruptive effects inevitable?", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "One side says technology decides and the disruption is coming whatever we do. The other says people decide, through laws, companies and choices. Debate in two groups, then vote. VOTE AFTER THE DEBATE"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 10, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "IBM's free Machine Learning and Deep Learning course shows how the models behind your feed are trained. It is course 3 of the AI Fundamentals badge. Finish it and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 11, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 4 · AI in Everyday Life", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Before Week 5, read TB Chapter 5 on large language models in education. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Everyday AI Recommendations Equity lenses Who benefits Bias cases Inevitable? Face unlock · maps · spam Mostly narrow AI Prediction from data 1. A “filter bubble” happens when… 2. Which is NOT one of the four equity lenses? 3. ProPublica's COMPAS analysis found that… 4. Why did Amazon's recruiting tool downgrade women's CVs? 5. The IMF (2024) estimated that about what share of jobs worldwide are exposed to AI? 6. The ATM example shows that…"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 0, "t": "Can you tell who wrote it?", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Read the two answers on the right and vote: which one did a chatbot write? If it is hard to tell, that is exactly why large language models are changing how students learn and how teachers assess. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 1, "t": "An LLM in one minute", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "A large language model predicts the next word, again and again, from patterns in huge amounts of text. It sounds fluent because it has seen so much language, but it does not check facts, so it can be confidently wrong. NEXT-WORD PREDICTION · ILLUSTRATIVE PROBABILITIES"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 2, "t": "Where LLMs enter education", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Students use LLMs as tutors, writing helpers and study planners. Teachers use them to draft materials and feedback. Institutions use them for support chatbots and admissions. Each use brings its own benefit and its own risk. STUDENTS · TEACHERS · INSTITUTIONS"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 3, "t": "The big promise: a tutor for everyone", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "In 1984 Benjamin Bloom found that students with a one-to-one tutor performed about two standard deviations better than students in a normal class. AI tutors promise that advantage at scale, if they guide students to think instead of giving them answers. BLOOM'S 2-SIGMA RESULT · NORMAL CURVES"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 4, "t": "Threats: what can go wrong", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "The same tools can harm learning: answers you did not think through, invented facts and sources, unfair detectors, privacy leaks, unequal access, and over-reliance that weakens your own skills. Open each threat on the right. SIX THREATS · OPEN EACH ONE"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 5, "t": "Microsoft Copilot as a tutor, not a ghost-writer", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Copilot is free with a Microsoft account. Ask it to explain this week's idea, then ask it to quiz you instead of giving answers. Compare the two chats: one outsources your thinking, the other trains it. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · MICROSOFT ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 6, "t": "NotebookLM: an AI that only reads your sources", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "NotebookLM answers only from the documents you upload and shows a citation for every claim. Upload this week's slides or chapter, ask three questions, and click the citations to check where each answer came from. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · GOOGLE ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 7, "t": "Watch: the case for AI tutors", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Sal Khan, founder of Khan Academy, argues that AI tutors could give every student the benefit of personal tutoring. Watch critically: which of today's threats does he address, and which does he skip? WATCH WATCH · TED · SAL KHAN"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 8, "t": "Case: even OpenAI gave up on its detector", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "In 2023 OpenAI withdrew its own tool for spotting AI-written text because of its low accuracy. A Stanford study found that seven popular detectors wrongly flagged over half of essays written by non-native English speakers. A detector score alone is not proof. REAL CASE IN THE NEWS · OPENAI · UPDATED 20 JULY 2023"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 9, "t": "Fact-check the AI tutor", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "This answer was written in the style of an AI tutor, and some sentences are false. Click the ones you think are wrong, then check your score. CLICK THE SENTENCES YOU THINK ARE FALSE"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 10, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Google's free Introduction to Generative AI and Introduction to Large Language Models courses explain the tools from today's lab. They lead to the Generative AI Fundamentals skill badge. Finish them and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 11, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 5 · Applications of AI: Education and Learning", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Before Week 6, read TB Chapter 3, Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, and start choosing your field for Assignment 2. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS LLM basics Opportunities Threats Detector case Principles Golden rules Predicts the next word Hallucination: confident but false 1. At its core, what does a large language model do when it writes? 2. A “hallucination” is… 3. Which is NOT one of the six threats? 4. In the Stanford study, AI detectors… 5. Georgia State's course chatbot worked well mainly because… 6. An AI tutor gives a confident answer with a reference. What should you do first?"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 0, "t": "Who should read your scan?", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Imagine your chest X-ray is waiting to be read. Vote: a doctor alone, the AI alone, or both together? Keep your answer; we test it against real evidence later today. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 1, "t": "AI along the patient journey", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "AI appears at every stage of care: triage, diagnosis, treatment planning, drug discovery, monitoring at home and hospital operations. Click each stage to see a real example. SIX STAGES · CLICK EACH"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 2, "t": "Diagnosis is image classification", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "A diagnostic model is the image classifier from Week 3, trained on thousands of labelled scans. It gives each new image a score, a threshold turns the score into a flag, and a clinician reviews the flagged cases and decides. TRAIN ONCE · USE MANY TIMES · HUMAN DECIDES"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 3, "t": "Missed cases or false alarms?", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Every screening model needs a threshold. Set it low and you catch more disease but raise many false alarms. Set it high and alarms fall but you miss sick patients. Move the slider and decide which mistake is worse. 40 ILLUSTRATIVE PATIENTS"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 4, "t": "From target to medicine", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Developing a new medicine usually takes many years. AI speeds up the early steps by predicting protein structures and which molecules might fit a target, so only the best candidates go to the lab and to clinical trials. FROM TARGET TO MEDICINE"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 5, "t": "Explore a protein structure predicted by AI", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "The AlphaFold Protein Structure Database holds over 200 million structures predicted by DeepMind's AlphaFold. Search for haemoglobin or insulin, rotate the 3D model, and read the colour code that shows how confident the prediction is. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 6, "t": "Watch a model's confidence drop", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Open Teachable Machine again and train two classes: an open hand and a fist. Then show a half-open hand. The confidence falls, which is exactly the case a medical threshold must handle. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 7, "t": "Watch: solving a 50-year-old puzzle", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Predicting how a protein folds was a grand challenge in biology for fifty years. Watch how the AlphaFold team approached it; its creators shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. WATCH WATCH · GOOGLE DEEPMIND"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 8, "t": "Case: the sepsis alert that missed most patients", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Epic's sepsis model was used in hundreds of US hospitals. When Michigan Medicine tested it independently in 2021, it caught only about a third of sepsis patients and alerted on almost one in five of all patients. Vendor claims need independent testing. EXTERNAL TEST · MICHIGAN MEDICINE A hidden proxy: cost instead of need Who is in the training images? Automation bias"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 9, "t": "When AI is wrong, who is responsible?", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "If an AI misses a cancer, who is to blame: the developer, the hospital, the doctor who trusted it, or the regulator who approved it? Divide the responsibility on the right and defend your split. DIVIDE THE RESPONSIBILITY"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 10, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Microsoft Learn's Introduction to computer vision module explains how models read images like the scans we studied today. Finish it, keep the trophy, and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 11, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 6 · Applications of AI: AI in Healthcare", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Before Week 7, read RB c, Chapter 1, and choose your field for Assignment 2. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Uses Diagnostics Errors Sepsis case Ethics pillars Human-in-the-loop Diagnosis · treatment Drug discovery · patient care Hospital operations 1. A false negative in medical AI means… 2. Diagnostic image AI is usually trained by… 3. “Human-in-the-loop” means… 4. In the external test of the Epic Sepsis Model, it… 5. “Alert fatigue” happens when… 6. AlphaFold is famous for…"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 0, "t": "2:47 a.m. Your card has been blocked", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Your bank has just blocked your card and sent you a message. No person looked at it: a model did. Vote on what triggered the block. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 1, "t": "Where AI works in finance and business", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Banks use AI to detect fraud, score credit and manage risk. Investors use it to build portfolios and trade. Businesses use it to forecast demand, set prices and answer customers. Click each use for an example. FINANCE · BUSINESS"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 2, "t": "Fraud detection is anomaly detection", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "A fraud model learns your normal pattern: usual amounts, places, times and shops. A payment that breaks several patterns at once gets a high score and is blocked. Change the signals on the right and watch the score. FICTIONAL CARD · 12 PAYMENTS WHEN MERCHANT AMOUNT SIGNALS RESULT Sun 09:12 Carrefour, Dubai AED 312 APPROVED Sun 13:40 ENOC, Dubai AED 140 APPROVED Mon 02:47 Electronics store, online (overseas) AED 4,850 BLOCKED FRAUD Mon 08:05 RTA Nol top-up AED 100 APPROVED Tue 20:15 Hotel, Istanbul AED 2,900 BLOCKED Tue 21:02 Restaurant, Istanbul AED 410 APPROVED Wed 03:30 Gift cards, online AED 1,500 BLOCKED FRAUD Wed 12:10 Talabat AED 68 APPROVED Thu 01:15 Taxi app, Dubai AED 54 APPROVED Thu 16:44 Gaming credits, overseas site AED 920 BLOCKED FRAUD Fri 11:30 Furniture store, Dubai (new sofa) AED 6,200 BLOCKED Sat 04:05 Crypto exchange, overseas AED 3,700 BLOCKED FRAUD"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 3, "t": "Credit scoring and the bias loop", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Credit models predict who will repay a loan from past data. If past lending was unfair, the model learns that unfairness, approves fewer people from the same groups, and creates new data that confirms it. That is the bias loop. THE BIAS LOOP"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 4, "t": "Don't put all your eggs in one basket", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Portfolio optimisation chooses a mix of investments that balances return and risk. Mixing assets that do not move together lowers risk without giving up much return. Move the slider between the two funds and find the safest mix. TWO FUNDS · ILLUSTRATIVE NUMBERS"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 5, "t": "How a robo-advisor works", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "A robo-advisor asks about your goals and comfort with risk, proposes a portfolio, and rebalances it automatically when markets move. It is cheap and consistent, but special situations still need a human adviser. HOW A ROBO-ADVISOR WORKS"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 6, "t": "Read demand like a data analyst", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Businesses forecast demand from search data. In Google Trends, compare searches for “umbrella” and “sunscreen” in the United Arab Emirates over five years, and describe the seasonal pattern a shop could plan for. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · NO ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 7, "t": "Make your first forecast", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Open a new Google Sheet, enter twelve months of sales, and let the FORECAST.LINEAR function predict month thirteen. Then add one unusual month and see how much a single outlier moves the prediction. TOOLS LAB FREE TOOL · GOOGLE ACCOUNT"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 8, "t": "Case: the Flash Crash of 6 May 2010", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "On 6 May 2010 US stock prices plunged and recovered within about half an hour. The official SEC and CFTC report found that one large automated sell order, executed fast and without regard to price, triggered a chain reaction among trading algorithms. FLASH CRASH · SKETCH"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 9, "t": "Watch: how algorithms shape our world", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Kevin Slavin shows how trading and pricing algorithms already run much of the economy, sometimes in ways nobody fully understands. Watch and note one example that surprised you. WATCH WATCH · TED · KEVIN SLAVIN"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 10, "t": "Risks and limits of AI in finance", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Finance AI can be biased, opaque, fooled by situations missing from its data, and dangerous when many firms use similar models at once. Open each risk and name one way to reduce it. SIX RISKS · OPEN EACH Herding Black box Systemic risk Data quality Bias Cost and skills"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 11, "t": "AI Nerd · take it further", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Microsoft Learn's Fundamentals of machine learning module explains regression, classification and clustering, the models behind today's fraud, credit and forecasting examples. Finish it and send your proof for a class-activity bonus."}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 12, "t": "Practise & remember", "w": "Week 7 · Applications of AI: AI in Finances and Business", "x": "Open each branch of the mind map, then answer the six questions. Assignment 2, “AI in my world”, is set this week: the brief, rubric and submission button are on the course page. The midterm is next week and covers Weeks 1 to 7. THE WEEK IN ONE MAP · THEN SIX QUESTIONS Uses Fraud Credit Portfolio Trading risks Big risks Fraud · credit · risk Portfolio · trading · robo-advice 1. Fraud detection is an example of… 2. Making a fraud detector stricter will usually… 3. A “proxy variable” is… 4. A 50/50 mix of the umbrella and sunscreen companies returns… 5. “Systemic risk” means… 6. Assignment 2 is assessed on relevance, examples and…"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 0, "t": "How to revise for the midterm", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "The midterm covers Weeks 1 to 7 and is worth 30% of your grade. This page collects everything you need: two summary tables, the four themes that repeat, flashcards for every key term, and practice questions with hints. REMEMBER FOR THE EXAM The answer formula for almost any question: define the term, give a real example, name a benefit, name a risk, and finish with human oversight. Marking rewards this structure. Revise each week's Exam takeaways scene and the two tables here. Self-test with the flashcards until you know them all. Practise the 14 questions aloud with a partner, then check the hints. Retake each week's exit check. THE ANSWER FORMULA"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 1, "t": "Weeks 1–4 in one table", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "If you can fill in this table from memory, you are ready for Weeks 1–4. Cover a column and test yourself. FOUNDATIONS OF AI WEEK 1 WEEK 2 WEEK 3 WEEK 4 Question What is AI? Where did it come from? How does it work? Who does it affect? Key idea Narrow AI, not general Hype, winters, springs Learning from data Impact depends on choices Key terms ANI/AGI, Turing Test Dartmouth, AI winter, deep learning ML types, transformer, RLHF Bias, equity, filter bubble Key example Chess vs tea-making Deep Blue vs AlphaGo Spam filter, ChatGPT COMPAS, ATMs"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 2, "t": "Comparing the three fields", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Your master memory aid for comparison questions. Practise saying one row across all three fields. EDUCATION · HEALTHCARE · FINANCE EDUCATION (WK 5) HEALTHCARE (WK 6) FINANCE (WK 7) Main AI use Tutoring, feedback, admin Diagnosis, drug discovery, care Fraud, credit, trading, advice Type of AI LLMs (language) Image models, prediction Anomaly detection, prediction Biggest benefit Personal learning at scale Earlier, faster detection Speed, lower cost, access Biggest risk Integrity, over-reliance Wrong or biased diagnosis Systemic and herding risk Case study AI detector bias Epic Sepsis Model Flash Crash / Knight Capital Human role Teacher + student judge Clinician decides Risk and compliance oversight"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 3, "t": "Four themes that repeat in every field", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "REMEMBER FOR THE EXAM Exam trick: for any application question, finish with these four themes. They fit education, health and finance. CLICK EACH THEME"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 4, "t": "Cheat sheet: key terms to memorise", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Read the cards first. Then switch on Self-test: tap a card to reveal the meaning, and tap again to mark it as known. Aim for all of them. 23 KEY TERMS · WEEKS 1–7"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 5, "t": "Practice questions · Weeks 1–4", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Try each question without notes, aloud or on paper. Then open the hint and compare. SEVEN QUESTIONS"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 6, "t": "Practice questions · Weeks 5–7", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Same method. For the discuss and essay questions, use the answer formula. SEVEN QUESTIONS"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 7, "t": "Mixed quiz · Weeks 1–7", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Ten questions across all seven weeks. Note the ones you miss and revisit that week's takeaways. TEN QUESTIONS 1. Machine learning can be summarised as… 2. AI was named as a field at… 3. The deep learning boom after 2012 came from… 4. “Attention” in a transformer… 5. Pre-training → fine-tuning → … 6. The four equity lenses are access, representation, outcomes and… 7. Which is NOT one of the six education threats? 8. A false negative in medical AI is… 9. Diversification lowers risk most when assets… 10. Removing a sensitive column (e.g. gender) from a credit model…"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 8, "t": "Before the midterm", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Revise: each week's Exam takeaways and the two tables. Practise: the 14 questions aloud with a partner. Remember the formula: define → example → benefit → risk → human oversight. Reminder: the midterm covers Weeks 1–7 and is worth 30% of the course grade. Questions? Bring them to office hours before the exam."}], "terms": [{"g": "AI", "d": "Systems doing tasks that normally need human intelligence"}, {"g": "Narrow / General AI", "d": "One task only (all AI today) / any task like a human (not yet)"}, {"g": "Turing Test", "d": "If you cannot tell it from a human in chat, it passes"}, {"g": "Chinese Room", "d": "Searle: correct answers do not prove understanding"}, {"g": "Symbolic AI", "d": "Hand-written IF-THEN rules"}, {"g": "AI winter", "d": "Period of cut funding after over-hyped promises"}, {"g": "Machine learning", "d": "Learns patterns from data instead of fixed rules"}, {"g": "Supervised / Unsupervised / Reinforcement", "d": "Labels / no labels, finds groups / rewards"}, {"g": "Neural network · weights", "d": "Layered units · adjustable connection strengths"}, {"g": "Transformer · token", "d": "Model using attention · piece of text"}, {"g": "Attention", "d": "Lets each word weigh which other words matter"}, {"g": "RLHF", "d": "Learning from human ranking of answers"}, {"g": "Filter bubble", "d": "The more you click, the narrower your feed"}, {"g": "Algorithmic bias", "d": "Systematically unfair results from skewed data or design"}, {"g": "LLM", "d": "Model that predicts the next word from huge text data"}, {"g": "Hallucination", "d": "Confident output that is false"}, {"g": "False positive / negative", "d": "False alarm / missed real case"}, {"g": "Human-in-the-loop", "d": "AI suggests, a human decides"}, {"g": "Alert fatigue", "d": "Too many false alarms, so people ignore them"}, {"g": "Anomaly detection", "d": "Learn what is normal; flag what is not"}, {"g": "Diversification", "d": "Spread investments to lower risk"}, {"g": "Systemic risk", "d": "One failure threatens the whole system"}, {"g": "Proxy variable", "d": "Neutral-looking feature that stands in for a sensitive one"}]};
